@Nyandragon Its a bit of a stretch to be discussing budget ~200ish dollar graphic cards in the same breath as private jets. Ofcourse if you only plan on playing old games indefinitely, then there isn't much reason to upgrade (still one can make a Power Saving argument for something like the 4060 with its prodigal 115w tdp and 110w avg game utilization, which in the games you play would probably use less than half the power of your Gtx 980 with its 165w tdp, since the Gpu utilization % will be much much lower on the 4060, so it can probably run them at your screens refresh rate for just a fraction of that wattage. That extra power use will amount to savings each year, which eventually can save enough money long term to justify it based on this advantage alone (this is at its most relevant in countries suffering from High energy prices).

Also the gpu is nearly a decade old, just about anything would be a monstrous upgrade over it. You can even find a used card in the ~$100 range that will crush it, and if somehow you can sell the Gtx 980 for any amount (even ~$50 would be great), you can further reduce the cost differential to the price of a family outing at a restaurant. Would be worth watching HUB monthly gpu pricing update, they go over Used card pricing month on month. Notable price points that i see from the May video released a few days ago are- 1) Rx 6600 at $155 2) Rtx 2060 at $153 3) Gtx 1660 at just $92 (!) 4) Gtx 1660 Super at $107 5) Rx 5700 at $133.

So ample good deals at the ~$100ish range for sizable performance uplift over the ancient gtx 980, if you are okay buying Used. Also all these prices will keep getting lower and lower.

